WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.950
In many episodes about computer graphics,
we explored works on how to simulate the motion

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:16.480
and the collision of different bodies. However,
sounds are just as important as visuals, and

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.829
there are really cool techniques out there
that take the geometry and material description

00:00:20.829 --> 00:00:26.649
of such objects and they simulate how smashing
them together would sound like.

00:00:26.649 --> 00:00:31.869
This one is a more sophisticated method which
is not only faster than previous works, but

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:37.269
can simulate a greater variety of materials,
and we can also edit the solutions without

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:42.460
needing to recompute the expensive equations
that yield the sound as a result.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:48.429
The faster part comes from a set of optimizations,
most importantly something that is called

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:55.249
mesh simplification. This means that the simulations
are done not on the original, but vastly simplified

00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:56.370
shapes.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:01.359
The result of this simplified simulation is
close to indistinguishable from the real deal,

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:04.790
but is considerably cheaper to compute.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.119
What is really cool is that the technique
also offers editing capabilities. You compute

00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:19.500
a simulation only once, and then, edit and
explore as much as you desire.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.159
The stiffness and damping parameters can be
edited without any additional work. Quite

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:39.119
a few different materials can be characterized
with this.

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:44.810
The model can also approximate a quite sophisticated
phenomenon where the frequency of a sound

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:51.250
is changing in time. One can, for instance,
specify stiffness values that vary in time

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.590
to produce these cool frequency shifting effects.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:59.950
It is also possible to exaggerate or dampen
different frequencies of these sound effects,

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:10.320
and the results are given to you immediately.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.450
This is meeting all my standards. Amazing
piece of work.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.110
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

